        "owner": "divinity-reply-records-desk",
        "field": "label"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1075",
    "text": "Is being hurt the same as doing wrong?\nWhat is the nature of evil?",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4670,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "objective",
        "owner": "wound-and-meal",
        "field": "objectives"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1076",
    "text": "Augustine explains evil as damage to, or loss of, something good. Compare a wound with stealing a meal: suffering harm is not the same as choosing to harm someone.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4671,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "entry fallback",
        "owner": "wound-and-meal",
        "field": "motivation"
      },
      {
        "row": 4673,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "story body",
        "owner": "wound-and-meal",
        "field": "entryCue.text"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1077",
    "text": "An injury is harm suffered; stealing a meal is harm chosen. Augustine treats both as losses of goodness, but distinguishes suffering from wrongdoing.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4672,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "completion/ending",
        "owner": "wound-and-meal",
        "field": "completionText"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1078",
    "text": "Augustine\u2019s study, North Africa, c. 421",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4674,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "story context heading",
        "owner": "wound-and-meal",
        "field": "entryCue.contextHeading"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1079",
    "text": "Why do we fail to do what we know is right?\nWhy would an infant need forgiveness?",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4675,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "objective",
        "owner": "promise-and-infant",
        "field": "objectives"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1080",
    "text": "It is evening. The desk has been cleared for a new study session about God's grace: help that is given rather than earned. Augustine asks why knowing what is right may not lead to doing it. Compare his answer with his separate claim about infants and inherited sin; illness is not evidence of a child's wrongdoing.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4676,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "entry fallback",
        "owner": "promise-and-infant",
        "field": "motivation"
      },
      {
        "row": 4678,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "story body",
        "owner": "promise-and-infant",
        "field": "entryCue.text"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1081",
    "text": "Augustine says an adult may need God's help to do what they know is right. He separately says infants need forgiveness because of Adam's sin, whether they are healthy or ill.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4677,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "completion/ending",
        "owner": "promise-and-infant",
        "field": "completionText"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1082",
    "text": "Does foreseeing harm make you responsible?\nDoes evil have a source of its own?",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4679,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "objective",
        "owner": "opposed-origins",
        "field": "objectives"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1083",
    "text": "The Book of Two Principles argues that good and evil have opposed sources. Reconstruct its reasoning and compare it with Augustine's account. Its central claim conflicts with Augustine\u2019s explanation of evil.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4680,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "entry fallback",
        "owner": "opposed-origins",
        "field": "motivation"
      },
      {
        "row": 4683,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "story body",
        "owner": "opposed-origins",
        "field": "entryCue.text"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1084",
    "text": "Augustine describes evil as damage within a good creation. The Book of Two Principles gives evil an opposed source. The accounts disagree about where evil comes from.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4681,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "completion/ending",
        "owner": "opposed-origins",
        "field": "completionText"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1085",
    "text": "Augustine describes evil as damage within a good creation. The Book of Two Principles gives evil an opposed source. The creator's responsibility remains in question.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4682,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "skip bridge",
        "owner": "opposed-origins",
        "field": "onAlreadyComplete.text"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1086",
    "text": "Northern Italy, thirteenth century",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4684,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "story context heading",
        "owner": "opposed-origins",
        "field": "entryCue.contextHeading"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1087",
    "text": "Whose fault is fire?\nWhat can reason tell us about God?",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4685,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "objective",
        "owner": "reason-and-fire",
        "field": "objectives"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1088",
    "text": "The next morning, the study desk is ready for a comparison with Aquinas. Compare Aquinas's fire example with the argument you just made. Fire can function as fire while harming something else. Consider what that challenges and which questions about suffering remain unanswered.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4686,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "entry fallback",
        "owner": "reason-and-fire",
        "field": "motivation"
      },
      {
        "row": 4688,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "story body",
        "owner": "reason-and-fire",
        "field": "entryCue.text"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1089",
    "text": "Aquinas says fire can function well as fire while harming something else. That challenges the claim that harm must come from a wholly evil source, but leaves the creator's responsibility in question.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4687,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "completion/ending",
        "owner": "reason-and-fire",
        "field": "completionText"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1090",
    "text": "Does being Son mean being less divine?\nHow can three be one God?",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4689,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "objective",
        "owner": "three-and-one",
        "field": "objectives"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1091",
    "text": "It is evening. We clear the desk to consider what Christians mean by the Trinity. Augustine's earlier text is now compared with Aquinas's limits on proof. Clarify the Christian claim of one God as Father, Son and Holy Spirit without treating human examples as proof of that belief.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4690,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "entry fallback",
        "owner": "three-and-one",
        "field": "motivation"
      },
      {
        "row": 4693,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "story body",
        "owner": "three-and-one",
        "field": "entryCue.text"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1092",
    "text": "Augustine distinguishes Father, Son and Holy Spirit while affirming one God. Aquinas says observation of created things cannot prove this belief.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4691,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "completion/ending",
        "owner": "three-and-one",
        "field": "completionText"
      },
      {
        "row": 4692,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "skip bridge",
        "owner": "three-and-one",
        "field": "onAlreadyComplete.text"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1093",
    "text": "What does this bodily rite tell us about creation?\nDoes rejecting worldly desires mean rejecting people?",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4694,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "objective",
        "owner": "hands-and-book",
        "field": "objectives"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1094",
    "text": "The Lyon ritual is a separate medieval text. Its ceremony uses hands and a Gospel book while claiming a spiritual gift. Examine what those physical actions do, and what they cannot establish about the community's beliefs about creation.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4695,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "entry fallback",
        "owner": "hands-and-book",
        "field": "motivation"
      },
      {
        "row": 4697,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "story body",
        "owner": "hands-and-book",
        "field": "entryCue.text"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1095",
    "text": "The Lyon rite uses hands and a Gospel book while promising a spiritual gift. The candidate also promises to reject worldly desires and love enemies. These commitments do not tell us everything the community believed about creation.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4696,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "completion/ending",
        "owner": "hands-and-book",
        "field": "completionText"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1096",
    "text": "How could water give an inward gift?\nAre these two rites saying the same thing?\nCan we really act when God helps us?",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4698,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "objective",
        "owner": "axe-and-water",
        "field": "objectives"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1097",
    "text": "The next morning, the desk is ready for a new comparison. Aquinas compares a religious rite with a tool through which someone acts. Use the axe example to clarify his account of baptism, while keeping separate his belief that God appointed this particular rite.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4699,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "entry fallback",
        "owner": "axe-and-water",
        "field": "motivation"
      },
      {
        "row": 4701,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "story body",
        "owner": "axe-and-water",
        "field": "entryCue.text"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1098",
    "text": "For Aquinas, God can act through baptism as a craftsperson acts through a tool. He also holds that God appointed this rite; ordinary water is not automatically a sacrament.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4700,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "completion/ending",
        "owner": "axe-and-water",
        "field": "completionText"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1099",
    "text": "What can tasting the bread establish?\nDo water and bread have the same religious role?",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4702,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "objective",
        "owner": "bread-and-body",
        "field": "objectives"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1100",
    "text": "It is evening. We return to the cleared study desk. Aquinas says Communion's bread becomes Christ's body while keeping its appearance and taste. How does he distinguish what a thing is from how it appears?",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4703,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "entry fallback",
        "owner": "bread-and-body",
        "field": "motivation"
      },
      {
        "row": 4706,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "story body",
        "owner": "bread-and-body",
        "field": "entryCue.text"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "uid": "C1101",
    "text": "Aquinas says the bread becomes Christ's body while its appearance and taste remain. A change in appearance would therefore not be the test of his claim.",
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "row": 4704,
        "world": "divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7",
        "surface": "completion/ending",
        "owner": "bread-and-body",
        "field": "completionText"
      }
    ]
  }
]